1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,111
Good evening, and welcome back to The Quiet Archive.

2
00:00:11,111 --> 00:00:15,381
Tonight's story is a slow one, and that is on purpose.

3
00:00:15,381 --> 00:00:18,306
You do not need to follow every word.

4
00:00:18,306 --> 00:00:21,548
If your thoughts wander, let them wander.

5
00:00:21,548 --> 00:00:26,925
If you fall asleep before the end, that is the best possible ending.

6
00:00:28,225 --> 00:00:31,524
Settle into the place where you are resting.

7
00:00:31,524 --> 00:00:34,824
Let the pillow take the weight of your head.

8
00:00:34,824 --> 00:00:43,073
Let your jaw loosen, and your forehead soften, and let one long breath leave your body, as slowly as it likes.

9
00:00:44,373 --> 00:00:55,629
Tonight we are going to spend an evening and a night inside a monastery, somewhere in the countryside of Europe, around eight or nine hundred years ago.

10
00:00:55,629 --> 00:01:00,147
A place built for prayer, for work, and above all, for quiet.

11
00:01:00,147 --> 00:01:07,626
A place where the day was measured not by clocks, but by bells, and by the slow turning of the light.

12
00:01:08,926 --> 00:01:12,015
We will arrive at the gate just before dusk.

13
00:01:12,015 --> 00:01:19,033
We will hear the evening prayers, share a simple supper, and walk the covered paths of the cloister.

14
00:01:19,033 --> 00:01:26,333
We will visit the room where books were copied by hand, and the garden where herbs and bees were tended.

15
00:01:26,333 --> 00:01:33,844
And when the last prayers of the day are sung, we will follow the monks into the deep silence of the night.

16
00:01:43,144 --> 00:01:49,108
The road to the monastery climbs gently through fields and low stone walls.

17
00:01:49,108 --> 00:01:57,457
The sun has already slipped behind the hills, and the sky is turning from pale gold to a soft, cool blue.

18
00:01:57,457 --> 00:02:05,330
Somewhere ahead, a single bell is ringing, slow and unhurried, calling everyone in from their work.

19
00:02:06,630 --> 00:02:11,842
The monastery itself sits in a sheltered valley, close to a stream.

20
00:02:11,842 --> 00:02:19,543
Monasteries were often built in places like this, near running water, away from the noise of towns.

21
00:02:19,543 --> 00:02:22,343
From the road you can see its shape.

22
00:02:22,343 --> 00:02:33,233
A long stone church with a square tower, a cluster of low buildings around it, an orchard, and the dark line of a wall that encloses it all.

23
00:02:34,533 --> 00:02:39,052
At the gate there is a small lodge, where the porter lives.

24
00:02:39,052 --> 00:02:44,567
He is an older monk, chosen for this job because he is patient and kind.

25
00:02:44,567 --> 00:02:56,975
The rule the monks follow says that the porter should be someone wise enough to receive a message and give a sensible answer, and steady enough not to wander off.

26
00:02:58,276 --> 00:03:03,716
That rule was written centuries earlier by a man named Benedict, in Italy.

27
00:03:03,716 --> 00:03:10,994
It is a short book, practical and gentle, describing how a community of monks should live together.

28
00:03:10,994 --> 00:03:18,713
How they should pray, how they should eat, how much they should sleep, how they should treat one another.

29
00:03:18,713 --> 00:03:23,492
Monasteries all over Europe followed it, and many still do today.

30
00:03:24,792 --> 00:03:34,091
One of the things the rule asks is that guests should be welcomed warmly, as if they were Christ himself arriving at the door.

31
00:03:34,091 --> 00:03:43,243
So when you knock, the porter opens the little window in the gate, looks out at you with a tired smile, and says a blessing.

32
00:03:43,243 --> 00:03:51,288
Then the heavy door swings open, and you step inside, out of the wide evening, into a smaller, quieter world.

33
00:03:52,587 --> 00:03:56,139
Inside the wall, everything seems to slow down.

34
00:03:56,139 --> 00:03:57,500
The path is swept.

35
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:00,749
A cat sits on a low wall, washing its paws.

36
00:04:00,749 --> 00:04:11,555
Somewhere a cart is being unloaded, and a lay brother, one of the working members of the community, is leading a tired horse toward the stable.

37
00:04:11,555 --> 00:04:13,294
The bell stops ringing.

38
00:04:13,294 --> 00:04:21,153
And in the stillness that follows, you can hear the stream running softly on the other side of the wall.

39
00:04:30,453 --> 00:04:35,839
The bell you heard was calling the monks to Vespers, the evening prayer.

40
00:04:35,839 --> 00:04:45,789
In the monastery, the day was divided into a series of short services, spread from the middle of the night to the end of the evening.

41
00:04:45,789 --> 00:04:52,298
Vespers came as the light was fading, and it was one of the most beautiful of them all.

42
00:04:53,598 --> 00:04:56,455
The church is cool inside, and dim.

43
00:04:56,455 --> 00:05:04,944
The last daylight comes through narrow windows high in the walls, and a few candles burn near the altar.

44
00:05:04,944 --> 00:05:14,740
The monks file in quietly and take their places in wooden stalls, facing one another across the choir, in two long rows.

45
00:05:16,039 --> 00:05:17,796
Then they begin to sing.

46
00:05:17,796 --> 00:05:23,506
It is a single melody, without harmony, rising and falling like a slow breath.

47
00:05:23,506 --> 00:05:26,580
This kind of singing is called plainchant.

48
00:05:26,580 --> 00:05:37,487
The words are psalms, ancient songs of praise and longing, sung one verse on this side of the choir, and the next verse answered from the other side.

49
00:05:37,487 --> 00:05:38,585
Back and forth.

50
00:05:38,585 --> 00:05:39,683
Back and forth.

51
00:05:39,683 --> 00:05:43,709
Like waves arriving on a shore and slipping away again.

52
00:05:45,009 --> 00:05:49,732
You do not need to understand the words to feel the shape of it.

53
00:05:49,732 --> 00:05:58,220
The voices fill the stone room and linger in the air after each phrase, as if the building itself is singing along.

54
00:05:58,220 --> 00:06:06,413
Over the years, many of these monks will have sung every one of the hundred and fifty psalms hundreds of times.

55
00:06:06,413 --> 00:06:09,734
They know them the way you know the way home.

56
00:06:11,034 --> 00:06:15,707
After Vespers, the community walks to the refectory for supper.

57
00:06:15,707 --> 00:06:19,267
This is a long hall with tables along the walls.

58
00:06:19,267 --> 00:06:20,676
The meal is simple.

59
00:06:20,676 --> 00:06:29,354
Bread, a dish of beans or lentils, some vegetables from the garden, perhaps cheese or fish, and a cup of wine or ale.

60
00:06:29,354 --> 00:06:41,370
The rule of Benedict allowed a modest amount to drink, and asked for two cooked dishes at the main meal, so that anyone who could not eat one might eat the other.

61
00:06:42,670 --> 00:06:44,918
Nobody talks during the meal.

62
00:06:44,918 --> 00:06:56,005
Instead, one monk stands at a wooden reading desk and reads aloud, from scripture or from the lives of saints, while the others eat in silence.

63
00:06:56,005 --> 00:06:58,098
His voice is calm and even.

64
00:06:58,098 --> 00:06:59,571
Spoons touch bowls.

65
00:06:59,571 --> 00:07:01,819
Bread is passed hand to hand.

66
00:07:01,819 --> 00:07:10,425
The candles burn low on the tables, and outside the windows, the sky turns from blue to the deep colour of ink.

67
00:07:11,725 --> 00:07:19,829
If a monk needs something during the meal, the salt or the water jug, he does not ask for it out loud.

68
00:07:19,829 --> 00:07:27,854
Monasteries developed simple hand signs for everyday things, so that the silence would not be broken.

69
00:07:27,854 --> 00:07:29,443
A gesture for bread.

70
00:07:29,443 --> 00:07:30,952
A gesture for fish.

71
00:07:30,952 --> 00:07:34,130
A gesture for please, and for thank you.

72
00:07:34,130 --> 00:07:38,818
Imagine a whole room speaking softly with only their hands.

73
00:07:48,118 --> 00:07:53,976
After supper, there is a little time before the last prayers of the day.

74
00:07:53,976 --> 00:07:56,743
Let us step out into the cloister.

75
00:07:56,743 --> 00:07:59,591
This is the heart of the monastery.

76
00:07:59,591 --> 00:08:09,110
A square garden, open to the sky, surrounded on all four sides by a covered walkway, held up by rows of stone arches.

77
00:08:10,410 --> 00:08:13,521
The church forms one side of the square.

78
00:08:13,521 --> 00:08:27,521
On the other sides are the chapter house, where the monks meet each morning, the refectory where we just ate, and above one range of buildings, the long dormitory where they sleep.

79
00:08:27,521 --> 00:08:33,898
Everything important in their daily life is gathered around this one quiet garden.

80
00:08:35,198 --> 00:08:40,145
The moon has risen now, and it shines down into the open square.

81
00:08:40,145 --> 00:08:47,410
Between the arches, the light falls in long pale shapes across the stone floor of the walkway.

82
00:08:47,410 --> 00:08:48,415
Arch, shadow.

83
00:08:48,415 --> 00:08:49,420
Arch, shadow.

84
00:08:49,420 --> 00:09:00,009
Walking slowly along the path, you pass from light into darkness and back into light, again and again, at the pace of your own breathing.

85
00:09:01,309 --> 00:09:09,329
In the middle of the garden, there is often a well, or a small fountain, or simply a square of grass.

86
00:09:09,329 --> 00:09:13,219
Some cloisters had a single tree at their centre.

87
00:09:13,219 --> 00:09:21,874
The idea was to create a picture of paradise, a garden enclosed and protected, where the mind could be still.

88
00:09:23,174 --> 00:09:28,101
During the day, the cloister walkways were busy with quiet work.

89
00:09:28,101 --> 00:09:33,951
Monks sat on stone benches along the walls to read, or to study, or to copy.

90
00:09:33,951 --> 00:09:40,186
Some cloisters had little wooden desks built into the arches, close to the light.

91
00:09:40,186 --> 00:09:44,111
Older monks taught younger ones their letters here.

92
00:09:44,111 --> 00:09:49,500
Others simply walked, slowly, thinking, or praying under their breath.

93
00:09:50,799 --> 00:09:54,290
Now, at the end of the day, it is almost empty.

94
00:09:54,290 --> 00:10:02,906
One monk is sitting on a bench in the corner with his hands folded in his sleeves, looking at nothing in particular.

95
00:10:02,906 --> 00:10:07,362
Another walks the length of one side, turns, and walks back.

96
00:10:07,362 --> 00:10:10,704
Their sandals make a soft sound on the stone.

97
00:10:10,704 --> 00:10:18,874
A breeze moves across the garden, and the moonlight trembles a little on the surface of the water in the well.

98
00:10:28,174 --> 00:10:34,925
Just off the cloister there is a room we should visit before it closes for the night.

99
00:10:34,925 --> 00:10:39,452
This is the scriptorium, the place where books were made.

100
00:10:39,452 --> 00:10:48,824
In a world without printing, every single book had to be copied by hand, letter by letter, line by line, page by page.

101
00:10:48,824 --> 00:10:53,113
A single large book might take one person many months.

102
00:10:54,413 --> 00:11:02,448
The pages were not made of paper, but of parchment, prepared from the skins of calves, sheep, or goats.

103
00:11:02,448 --> 00:11:13,213
The skin was soaked, scraped, stretched on a frame, and dried, until it became a thin, smooth, pale surface that would last for centuries.

104
00:11:13,213 --> 00:11:17,816
The very finest kind, made from calfskin, is called vellum.

105
00:11:19,115 --> 00:11:25,537
The ink was often made from oak galls, small round growths that form on oak trees.

106
00:11:25,537 --> 00:11:31,175
They were crushed and soaked, and mixed with a little iron salt and gum.

107
00:11:31,175 --> 00:11:38,614
The ink went onto the page a pale brown, and slowly darkened to a deep, rich black as it dried.

108
00:11:38,614 --> 00:11:45,270
Many of these pages are still perfectly legible today, nearly a thousand years later.

109
00:11:46,570 --> 00:11:51,770
The scribes wrote with quills cut from the feathers of geese or swans.

110
00:11:51,770 --> 00:12:03,136
A good scribe kept a small knife beside him, to sharpen the quill as it wore down, and to gently scrape away a mistake from the surface of the parchment.

111
00:12:03,136 --> 00:12:10,788
In the margins of some old manuscripts you can still find little notes the scribes wrote to themselves.

112
00:12:10,788 --> 00:12:12,051
My hand is tired.

113
00:12:12,051 --> 00:12:13,240
The ink is thin.

114
00:12:13,240 --> 00:12:14,725
The light is fading.

115
00:12:14,725 --> 00:12:17,994
Thank goodness, it will soon be supper time.

116
00:12:19,294 --> 00:12:25,383
Tonight, the last scribe is finishing his work by the light of a single candle.

117
00:12:25,383 --> 00:12:28,543
He has copied his final line for the day.

118
00:12:28,543 --> 00:12:34,016
He wipes the quill, sets it down in its holder, and covers the inkwell.

119
00:12:34,016 --> 00:12:45,577
He looks over the page once more, the neat rows of letters, the space left for a painted capital that another brother will add later in gold and blue.

120
00:12:46,877 --> 00:12:53,554
Then he lays a cloth over the page to protect it, and stands, and stretches his stiff back.

121
00:12:53,554 --> 00:12:56,635
The book will wait for him until tomorrow.

122
00:12:56,635 --> 00:13:02,138
It has already waited for months, and it will wait patiently for many more.

123
00:13:02,138 --> 00:13:03,826
There is no hurry here.

124
00:13:03,826 --> 00:13:09,255
The work is slow, and steady, and it will be finished when it is finished.

125
00:13:10,556 --> 00:13:17,855
Along the walls of the room stand wooden chests and cupboards, where the finished books are kept.

126
00:13:17,855 --> 00:13:21,994
Each one represents months of quiet, careful attention.

127
00:13:21,994 --> 00:13:27,863
Lives of saints, books of psalms, works of history, of medicine, of astronomy.

128
00:13:27,863 --> 00:13:39,377
Many of the books that survive from the ancient world reached us because some patient scribe, in a room very much like this one, copied them out by hand.

129
00:13:48,677 --> 00:13:54,536
Beyond the cloister, through a little gate, lies the infirmary garden.

130
00:13:54,536 --> 00:14:02,571
Monasteries cared for their own sick and elderly, and often for travellers and local people too.

131
00:14:02,571 --> 00:14:08,346
And so they grew herbs, in neat raised beds, divided by narrow paths.

132
00:14:09,646 --> 00:14:14,889
In the dark, you cannot see the plants clearly, but you can smell them.

133
00:14:14,889 --> 00:14:20,058
Brush your hand along the edge of a bed, and the air fills with scent.

134
00:14:20,058 --> 00:14:20,428
Sage.

135
00:14:20,428 --> 00:14:21,092
Rosemary.

136
00:14:21,092 --> 00:14:21,462
Mint.

137
00:14:21,462 --> 00:14:22,126
Lavender.

138
00:14:22,126 --> 00:14:22,569
Thyme.

139
00:14:22,569 --> 00:14:27,886
Each herb had its uses, written down carefully in the monastery's books.

140
00:14:27,886 --> 00:14:37,486
Some for the stomach, some for coughs, some for sleep, and some simply because their fragrance made the sick room a gentler place.

141
00:14:38,786 --> 00:14:49,305
There is a famous old plan of an ideal monastery, drawn more than a thousand years ago, which still survives in a library in Switzerland.

142
00:14:49,305 --> 00:14:58,365
It shows every building in its place, and it even lists the herbs to be planted in the physician's garden, bed by bed.

143
00:14:58,365 --> 00:15:01,896
Lilies, roses, sage, rue, cumin, fennel, mint.

144
00:15:01,896 --> 00:15:09,037
Someone, long ago, sat down and imagined the whole peaceful place, right down to its flowers.

145
00:15:10,337 --> 00:15:17,940
At the edge of the garden stand a row of beehives, made of woven straw, shaped like round domes.

146
00:15:17,940 --> 00:15:20,632
Bees were precious to a monastery.

147
00:15:20,632 --> 00:15:23,800
Their honey sweetened food and medicine.

148
00:15:23,800 --> 00:15:34,017
And their wax made the best candles, which burned brightly and cleanly, with a soft, sweet smell, in the church and on the altar.

149
00:15:35,317 --> 00:15:38,990
At this hour, the bees are all inside, resting.

150
00:15:38,990 --> 00:15:46,101
If you lean close to one of the hives, you can hear a faint, low hum, like a sleeping town.

151
00:15:46,101 --> 00:15:56,416
Thousands of tiny lives, all gathered together in the warm dark, waiting for the morning sun to call them out again into the fields.

152
00:15:57,716 --> 00:16:03,089
The monk who looks after the bees has finished his work for the day.

153
00:16:03,089 --> 00:16:10,834
He walks slowly back along the garden path toward the church, his hands smelling of wax and herbs.

154
00:16:10,834 --> 00:16:13,679
Above him, the stars are very clear.

155
00:16:13,679 --> 00:16:19,369
A night bird calls from the orchard, once, twice, and then falls silent.

156
00:16:28,669 --> 00:16:31,312
Now the bell rings one more time.

157
00:16:31,312 --> 00:16:35,957
This is the call to Compline, the last service of the day.

158
00:16:35,957 --> 00:16:39,562
The word comes from the Latin for completion.

159
00:16:39,562 --> 00:16:46,930
It is the prayer that completes the day, and gently closes it, like the last page of a book.

160
00:16:48,230 --> 00:16:51,464
The monks gather in the church once more.

161
00:16:51,464 --> 00:16:57,380
Only a few candles are lit now, and most of the building is in deep shadow.

162
00:16:57,380 --> 00:17:07,004
Compline is short, and it is the same almost every night, so the monks know it by heart and barely need the light to read.

163
00:17:08,304 --> 00:17:13,244
The psalms they sing are about rest, and protection, and trust.

164
00:17:13,244 --> 00:17:23,754
Ancient words about lying down in peace and sleeping, about being sheltered through the night, about not being afraid of the darkness.

165
00:17:23,754 --> 00:17:25,871
The melody is soft and low.

166
00:17:25,871 --> 00:17:30,106
Some of the older monks close their eyes as they sing.

167
00:17:31,406 --> 00:17:39,287
Near the end, there is often a final song to the Virgin Mary, gentle and slow, sung in the near dark.

168
00:17:39,287 --> 00:17:51,460
Then the abbot gives a blessing, and the candles are put out, one by one, until only a single small light remains, burning near the altar through the night.

169
00:17:52,759 --> 00:17:56,908
And then begins what the monks call the Great Silence.

170
00:17:56,908 --> 00:18:01,902
From the end of Compline until the morning, no one speaks at all.

171
00:18:01,902 --> 00:18:02,747
Not a word.

172
00:18:02,747 --> 00:18:11,966
The rule of Benedict says that the monks should keep silence at all times, but especially during the hours of the night.

173
00:18:11,966 --> 00:18:16,269
Whatever was left unsaid today will wait until tomorrow.

174
00:18:17,569 --> 00:18:21,076
There is something very freeing in that.

175
00:18:21,076 --> 00:18:23,443
No conversations to finish.

176
00:18:23,443 --> 00:18:25,459
No questions to answer.

177
00:18:25,459 --> 00:18:27,300
No decisions to make.

178
00:18:27,300 --> 00:18:31,245
The day is over, and it has been handed back.

179
00:18:31,245 --> 00:18:35,980
Everyone simply turns, and walks quietly toward sleep.

180
00:18:37,279 --> 00:18:40,157
Let that silence settle over you too.

181
00:18:40,157 --> 00:18:45,525
Whatever was left unfinished in your own day can wait until tomorrow.

182
00:18:45,525 --> 00:18:49,569
For now, there is nothing to say, and nothing to do.

183
00:18:49,569 --> 00:18:56,181
There is only the quiet, and the dark, and the slow, steady rhythm of your breathing.

184
00:19:05,481 --> 00:19:14,269
The monks sleep together in one long room, the dormitory, up on the first floor of the building beside the church.

185
00:19:14,269 --> 00:19:24,830
The rule asked that they sleep in one place if possible, each in his own simple bed, with a light kept burning in the room until morning.

186
00:19:26,130 --> 00:19:27,612
The beds are plain.

187
00:19:27,612 --> 00:19:33,460
A wooden frame, a mattress stuffed with straw, a woollen blanket, a pillow.

188
00:19:33,460 --> 00:19:41,413
The monks sleep in their robes, so that they are ready to rise as soon as the bell rings in the night.

189
00:19:41,413 --> 00:19:48,509
The room is cool, and very quiet, and smells faintly of wool and wood smoke and candle wax.

190
00:19:49,809 --> 00:19:57,619
At one end of the dormitory there is a doorway that leads to a staircase, going straight down into the church.

191
00:19:57,619 --> 00:19:59,821
This is called the night stair.

192
00:19:59,821 --> 00:20:08,838
It meant that the monks could walk from their beds to the choir in the middle of the night without going outside into the cold.

193
00:20:08,838 --> 00:20:18,566
In a few old abbeys in England, these night stairs still survive, their stone steps worn into gentle hollows by centuries of sleepy feet.

194
00:20:19,866 --> 00:20:22,332
One by one, the monks lie down.

195
00:20:22,332 --> 00:20:25,832
A few whispered prayers, said without sound.

196
00:20:25,832 --> 00:20:29,094
A blanket pulled up against the cool air.

197
00:20:29,094 --> 00:20:34,503
Someone coughs softly at the far end of the room, and then is still.

198
00:20:34,503 --> 00:20:43,333
The single small lamp burns steadily on its ledge, and its light flickers very gently on the plain white walls.

199
00:20:44,633 --> 00:20:51,170
Through the narrow window, you can see a slice of the night sky, and a few bright stars.

200
00:20:51,170 --> 00:20:58,449
The moon has moved across the cloister by now, and its light is falling on the roof of the church.

201
00:20:58,449 --> 00:21:00,752
The whole monastery is resting.

202
00:21:00,752 --> 00:21:02,535
The bees in their hives.

203
00:21:02,535 --> 00:21:04,318
The horse in the stable.

204
00:21:04,318 --> 00:21:06,249
The books in their chests.

205
00:21:06,249 --> 00:21:08,032
The herbs in their beds.

206
00:21:08,032 --> 00:21:11,449
The stream, running on and on beyond the wall.

207
00:21:20,749 --> 00:21:21,657
Hours pass.

208
00:21:21,657 --> 00:21:24,216
The stars turn slowly overhead.

209
00:21:24,216 --> 00:21:31,068
And then, deep in the night, long before dawn, a small bell rings in the dormitory.

210
00:21:31,068 --> 00:21:38,498
It is time for the night office, the long prayer in the dark that the monks called Vigils.

211
00:21:39,798 --> 00:21:41,632
They rise without a word.

212
00:21:41,632 --> 00:21:49,627
They slip their feet into their shoes, and one after another, they walk down the night stair into the church.

213
00:21:49,627 --> 00:21:52,414
The candles are lit again, just a few.

214
00:21:52,414 --> 00:22:02,902
And in the depths of the night, while the whole countryside sleeps, the plainchant begins once more, slow and low, filling the dark stone room.

215
00:22:04,202 --> 00:22:08,783
For the monks, this was one of the most precious hours.

216
00:22:08,783 --> 00:22:11,948
The world outside was entirely asleep.

217
00:22:11,948 --> 00:22:14,114
No travellers on the road.

218
00:22:14,114 --> 00:22:14,864
No carts.

219
00:22:14,864 --> 00:22:15,697
No market.

220
00:22:15,697 --> 00:22:23,359
Only the quiet voices in the candlelight, keeping watch on behalf of everyone who could not.

221
00:22:24,660 --> 00:22:47,209
And when the office is over, the monks climb the night stair again, and return to their beds, and sleep a little longer, until the first grey light appears in the east, and the birds in the orchard begin to stir, and a new day starts, exactly like the last one, simple and slow and quiet.

222
00:22:48,509 --> 00:22:51,679
But that morning is still a long way off.

223
00:22:51,679 --> 00:22:54,695
For now, it is the middle of the night.

224
00:22:54,695 --> 00:22:58,097
The candles in the church have been put out.

225
00:22:58,097 --> 00:23:01,035
The cloister is silver with moonlight.

226
00:23:01,035 --> 00:23:08,690
And somewhere, in a quiet room, someone is keeping a small flame alive, so that you do not have to.

227
00:23:09,990 --> 00:23:11,556
You can let go now.

228
00:23:11,556 --> 00:23:15,019
Let the silence of the monastery hold you.

229
00:23:15,019 --> 00:23:18,564
Let the slow chant fade into the stillness.

230
00:23:18,564 --> 00:23:23,345
Let the night carry you gently, all the way until morning.

231
00:23:24,645 --> 00:23:29,470
Thank you for spending this evening with The Quiet Archive.

232
00:23:29,470 --> 00:23:30,288
Rest well.

233
00:23:30,288 --> 00:23:31,351
Sleep deeply.

234
00:23:31,351 --> 00:23:32,496
And goodnight.
